amorphic rock characterized by an abundance of platy minerals. In this example, the rock has prominent sillimanite porphyroblasts as large as 3 cm (1.2 in).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASillimanite-199671.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Hubnerite-Quartz-216455.jpg/330px-Hubnerite-Quartz-216455.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Hubnerite-Quartz-216455.jpg/330px-Hubnerite-Quartz-216455.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hübnerite, the manganese-rich end-member of the wolframite series, with minor quartz in the background","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHubnerite-Quartz-216455.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Kaolinite-Orthoclase-lw18c.jpg/330px-Kaolinite-Orthoclase-lw18c.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Kaolinite-Orthoclase-lw18c.jpg/330px-Kaolinite-Orthoclase-lw18c.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"When minerals react, the products will sometimes assume the shape of the reagent; the product mineral is termed a pseudomorph of (or after) the reagent. Illustrated here is a pseudomorph of kaolinite after orthoclase. Here, the pseudomorph preserved the Carlsbad twinning common in orthoclase.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKaolinite-Orthoclase-lw18c.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Topaz-235220.jpg/500px-Topaz-235220.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Topaz-235220.jpg/500px-Topaz-235220.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Topaz has a characteristic orthorhombic elongated crystal shape.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATopaz-235220.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Spinel-4mb4c.jpg/330px-Spinel-4mb4c.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Spinel-4mb4c.jpg/330px-Spinel-4mb4c.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Contact twins, as seen in spinel","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpinel-4mb4c.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Rough_diamond.jpg/330px-Rough_diamond.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Rough_diamond.jpg/330px-Rough_diamond.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Diamond is the hardest natural material, and has a Mohs hardness of 10.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARough_diamond.jpg"}],"sounds":{"mineral water":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b1/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-mineral_water.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/b/b1/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-mineral_water.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-mineral_water.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/b/b1/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-mineral_water.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-mineral_water.wav.ogg"}]}}}